## Cache Invalidation Strategies

- **Explicit invalidation**: Proactively delete cache keys when data changes
- **TTL-based**: Allow cache entries to expire naturally based on time-to-live
- **Write-through**: Update both cache and database simultaneously

## Implementation

```javascript
// Cache-aside with explicit invalidation
async function updateProfile(userId, data) {
  await redis.del(`user:${userId}`);
  await db.users.update(userId, data);
  return await getUserProfile(userId);
}
```

## Trade-offs

- **Explicit invalidation**: Provides immediate consistency but requires additional coordination
- **TTL-based**: Simpler to implement with eventual consistency guarantees
- **Write-through**: Optimal for read-heavy workloads but can increase write latency

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates the ability to design and align schemas across multiple API protocols, with particular focus on cursor-based pagination semantics and streaming patterns.

## Key Concepts

- Unified contract registry with synchronized schema definitions
- Cursor-based pagination with consistent after/page semantics
- Field projection and partial responses
- Protocol-specific adapters for per-protocol mapping
- Schema versioning and contract compatibility tests
- Streaming response patterns (gRPC)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Unified SearchQuery type
interface SearchQuery {
  filters: Record<string, any>;
  pagination: {
    cursor?: string;
    limit: number;
  };
  projection: string[];
  sort: SortOrder[];
}

// Protocol adapters normalize requests to canonical format
class ProtocolAdapter {
  normalizeRestRequest(body: RestSearchRequest): SearchQuery;
  normalizeGraphQLRequest(args: GraphQLSearchArgs): SearchQuery;
  normalizeGrpcRequest(req: SearchRequest): SearchQuery;
